# Nulang Grammar (EBNF)

> **Authoritative grammar for the Frozen (Core) and Stable tiers** of the
> Nulang language, per RFC 0002 (Frozen Core) and `GOVERNANCE.md` §2. This
> file is a Frozen-tier artifact: the Core productions below may not change
> without a new RFC and a major-version bump. The Stable productions may
> change only under the deprecation contract.
>
> The hand-rolled recursive-descent parser in `src/parser.rs` is the reference
> implementation of this grammar. The conformance corpus in
> `conformance/grammar/` (positive + negative examples) validates the parser
> against this grammar; see `conformance/README.md` for the check harness.
>
> **Conformance-suite relationship.** Every positive case in
> `conformance/grammar/positive_*.nula` must be in the language of §2 ∪ §3
> (modulo Experimental §4), and every negative case must fall outside it.
> Where the corpus and this grammar disagreed, the reconciliations are
> recorded in `docs/SPEC_RECONCILIATION.md`; the drift log in
> `conformance/grammar/README.md` lists the parser leniencies (accepted
> surface this grammar deliberately does NOT admit) and the parser bugs
> scheduled for fixing. Productions below carry `(* drift ... *)` comments
> at each reconciled site.
>
> Experimental surface (actors' `@backend`, `workflow`, `agent`, FFI `extern`,
> Python interop, WIT) is **out of scope** for this grammar and is not part of
> the frozen or stable language.
>
> EBNF conventions: `{ X }` = zero or more; `[ X ]` = optional; `X | Y` =
> alternation; `"tok"` = literal terminal; `(* comment *)`. Newlines and
> semicolons are equivalent statement separators (`SEP`); the parser also
> tolerates `,` between arms in `match`/`catch`/`receive` blocks and
> trailing commas in comma-separated lists (drift leniency 15 — tolerated,
> not normative).

## 1. Lexical conventions

```
IDENT        = lowercase ( lowercase | uppercase | digit | "_" )*
UPPER_IDENT  = uppercase ( lowercase | uppercase | digit | "_" )*
             (* the parser accepts either case in most name positions; the
                case distinction is a naming convention, enforced only for
                variant constructors in `type` bodies and (normatively)
                effect qualifiers — drift leniency 12 *)
INT_LIT      = digit { digit } | "0x" hex_digit { hex_digit }
             (* radix prefixes `0b`/`0o` and `_` digit separators are NOT
                supported. Known lexer deviation (drift leniency 10, parser
                fix scheduled): `0b1010`, `0o755`, `1_000` are silently
                mis-lexed (e.g. as `0` followed by an identifier) instead
                of producing a lex error. Do not rely on this. *)
FLOAT_LIT    = digit { digit } "." digit { digit } [ ("e"|"E") ["+"|"-"] digit { digit } ]
STRING_LIT   = '"' { char_escape | interpolation | printable_char - '"' - "#" } '"'
interpolation = "#{" expr "}" ;
             (* string interpolation is written "#{ expr }"; the forms
                f"...", """...""" and f"""...""" lex identically.
                Known deviation (drift leniency 17, parser fix scheduled):
                only the first expression inside `#{ ... }` is parsed. *)
BOOL_LIT     = "true" | "false"
(* There is no CHAR_LIT: character literals are Planned (SPEC2 §2.5.4) and
   the lexer rejects single-quoted literals — drift round 1. *)
```

Reserved keywords (not usable as identifiers), per `src/lexer.rs`
`keyword()`: `actor`, `agent`, `alias`, `and`, `as`, `ask`, `await`,
`behavior`, `box`, `break`, `case`, `catch`, `class`, `compensate`,
`consume`, `crdt`, `database`, `defer`, `durable`, `effect`, `else`,
`emit`, `entity`, `errdefer`, `event_sourced`, `except`, `extern`, `fail`,
`false`, `fn`, `for`, `given`, `handle`, `handler`, `hide`, `if`,
`impl`, `import`, `in`, `initial`, `iso`, `let`, `linear`, `lineariso`,
`link`, `local`, `match`, `migrate`, `module`, `monitor`, `nil`, `not`,
`opaque`, `or`, `organization`, `par`, `parallel`, `perform`,
`persistent`, `pub`, `receive`, `rec`, `recover`, `ref`, `remote`,
`requires`, `ensures`, `resume`, `return`, `seal`, `self`, `send`,
`spawn`, `state`, `state_machine`, `step`, `tag`, `then`, `throws`,
`trn`, `true`, `type`, `unit`, `until`, `using`, `val`, `var`, `while`,
`with`, `workflow`. (`after`, `version`, `events`, `apply`, `migration`,
`implements` are contextual keywords, special only in fixed positions.)

## 2. Core productions (Frozen — RFC 0002)

A Core program is a sequence of declarations. Core excludes actors, all
effects except `IO.print`/`IO.read`, and all capabilities except `val`.

```
program        = { ( decl | stmt ) SEP } ;
decl           = fn_decl
                | type_decl          (* enum / struct / variant *)
                | alias_decl
                | const_decl
                | import_decl
                | module_decl ;

fn_decl        = [ "pub" ] "fn" IDENT [ type_params ] "(" [ param_list ] ")"
                 [ "->" type ] [ sig_suffix ] block ;
                 (* return-type sigil is "->" (TokenKind::Arrow);
                    src/parser.rs parse_function, consume_if(Arrow) at :654.
                    A "->" with no type after it is REJECTED in fn
                    declarations (drift leniency 21: lambdas differ). *)
sig_suffix     = ( "!" | "throws" ) ( error_type | effect_row )
                 [ ( "!" | "throws" ) effect_row ] [ ":" cap ]
                 [ "requires" expr ] [ "ensures" expr ] ;
error_type     = type ;              (* resolved as a type name at parse time *)
                 (* In fn-signature position, `! T` with a non-"{" token
                    parses T as an ERROR TYPE, while `! { ... }` is an
                    effect row. Inside a nested arrow type, `!` always
                    introduces an effect row. Same surface token, two
                    grammars depending on position — drift item 5;
                    src/parser.rs:894-917 vs parse_type_arrow. *)
effect_row     = "{" ( [ IDENT { "," IDENT } [ "," "|" IDENT ] ]
                     | "|" IDENT ) "}"
                | IDENT ;
                 (* the row bar after an effect list REQUIRES a preceding
                    comma: `{IO, | r}` is legal, `{IO | r}` is rejected —
                    drift item 4, src/parser.rs parse_effect_row :5588.
                    Bare `{| r}` and `{r}` are accepted. *)

import_decl    = "import" IDENT { "::" IDENT } ;
                 (* `::`-separated paths only; `.` yields a dedicated
                    diagnostic (drift item 24) *)
module_decl    = "module" IDENT "{" { decl SEP } "}" ;

param_list     = param { "," param } [ "," ] ;
param          = [ cap ] IDENT [ ":" type ] [ "=" expr ] ;
                 (* capabilities on parameters are PREFIX (`val x: Int`) —
                    the postfix form `x: Int @ val` is rejected
                    (drift round 1 / item 1; src/parser.rs
                    try_parse_param_capability :5871) *)

type_decl      = [ "pub" ] [ "opaque" ] "type" UPPER_IDENT [ type_params ]
                  ( variant_body | record_body | alias_body ) ;
type_params    = "[" IDENT { "," IDENT } [ "," ] "]" ;
variant_body   = [ "|" ] variant { "|" variant } ;
variant        = UPPER_IDENT [ "(" type ")" ] ;
                 (* a constructor payload is a SINGLE type; a constructor
                    carrying several values takes a tuple payload
                    (drift round 1) *)
record_body    = "{" { field ( SEP | "," ) } "}" ;
alias_body     = "=" type ;          (* non-variant body desugars to an alias *)
field          = IDENT ":" type ;
alias_decl     = [ "pub" ] "type" "alias" UPPER_IDENT [ type_params ] "=" type ;
                 (* bare `alias Name = T` is REJECTED — the `type` keyword
                    is required (drift round 1; src/parser.rs
                    parse_type_alias :2159) *)
const_decl     = [ "pub" ] "const" IDENT [ ":" type ] "=" expr ;
                 (* known deviation (drift leniency 22, parser fix
                    scheduled): `const = 1` without a name currently
                    parses *)

(* Expressions — Pratt precedence; listed lowest to highest binding.
   Mirrors src/parser.rs :30-46. NOTE the quirk (SPEC2 Appendix E item 3):
   shift/bitwise operators bind TIGHTER than arithmetic. *)
expr           = assign_expr ;
assign_expr    = pipe_expr [ "=" assign_expr ] ;
                 (* `=` is accepted on any expression at parse time; lvalue
                    checking is not syntactic (drift leniency 16).
                    `+=` / `-=` are lexed but rejected at parse time
                    ("Not a binary operator") — drift round 1. *)
pipe_expr      = range_expr { "|>" IDENT [ "." IDENT ] } ;
                 (* desugars to a call with the LHS as first argument *)
range_expr     = or_expr [ ".." or_expr ] ;
or_expr        = and_expr { ( "||" | "or" ) and_expr } ;
and_expr       = eq_expr { ( "&&" | "and" ) eq_expr } ;
eq_expr        = cmp_expr { ( "==" | "!=" ) cmp_expr } ;
cmp_expr       = add_expr { ( "<" | ">" | "<=" | ">=" ) add_expr } ;
add_expr       = mul_expr { ( "+" | "-" ) mul_expr } ;
mul_expr       = shift_expr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) shift_expr } ;
shift_expr     = bitand_expr { ( "<<" | ">>" ) bitand_expr } ;
bitand_expr    = bitxor_expr { "&" bitxor_expr } ;
bitxor_expr    = bitor_expr { "^" bitor_expr } ;
bitor_expr     = exp_expr { "|||" exp_expr } ;
                 (* bitwise-or is `|||`; single `|` is reserved for
                    arm/pattern syntax (drift round 1) *)
exp_expr       = unary_expr [ "**" exp_expr ] ;   (* right-associative *)
unary_expr     = ( "-" | "!" | "not" | "&" | "*" ) unary_expr | postfix_expr ;

postfix_expr   = primary { "(" [ expr { "," expr } [ "," ] ] ")"   (* call *)
                        | "[" expr "]"                             (* index *)
                        | "." ( IDENT | INT_LIT )                  (* field/tuple *)
                        | ( "!" | "<-" ) IDENT "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"
                                               (* postfix send / async tell *)
                        | "?" [ "." IDENT ] } ;  (* try / nil-safe chaining *)
                 (* all send forms desugar to the same Expr::Send node;
                    there is no `send a to b` production *)

primary        = INT_LIT | FLOAT_LIT | STRING_LIT | BOOL_LIT
                | "nil" | "unit"
                | IDENT | UPPER_IDENT      (* variant constructors are values *)
                | "self"
                | "(" expr { "," expr } ")"            (* paren or tuple *)
                | block
                | if_expr
                | match_expr
                | lambda
                | record_literal
                | for_expr
                | while_expr ;

record_literal = "{" { field_init SEP } "}" ;
field_init     = IDENT [ ":" expr ] ;                     (* `x` desugars to `x: x` *)

if_expr        = "if" expr ( "then" ( block | expr ) | expr )
                 [ "else" ( if_expr | block | expr ) ] ;
                 (* `then` is optional for an expression then-branch but
                    REQUIRED when the then-branch is a block:
                    `if c { ... }` is rejected (drift round 1;
                    src/parser.rs parse_if :3563-3577).
                    `if let pattern = expr block [ else ... ]` desugars to
                    `match` (parse_if :3519). *)
match_expr     = "match" expr [ "with" ] "{" { match_arm ( SEP | "," ) } "}" ;
match_arm      = [ "case" | "|" ] pattern [ "if" expr ] "=>" expr ;
lambda         = "fn" "(" [ param_list ] ")" [ "->" [ type ] [ sig_suffix ] ] block ;
                 (* in lambda position `->` does NOT require a type
                    (drift leniency 21) *)
block          = "{" { stmt [ SEP ] } [ expr ] "}" ;
stmt           = let_stmt | var_stmt | return_stmt | "break" | expr ;
let_stmt       = "let" IDENT [ ":" type ] "=" expr ;
                 (* NO capability annotations on `let`: both
                    `let x: Int @ val = ...` and `let val x = ...` are
                    rejected (drift item 2). `let rec f(params) = body`
                    and `let x = a in b` forms also parse. *)
var_stmt       = "var" IDENT [ ":" type ] "=" expr ;
return_stmt    = "return" [ expr ] ;
for_expr       = "for" IDENT "in" expr expr ;
while_expr     = "while" expr expr ;

(* Patterns *)
pattern        = or_pattern ;
or_pattern     = alias_pattern { "|" alias_pattern } ;
                 (* or-patterns desugar to duplicated arms; a trailing
                    `|` is rejected *)
alias_pattern  = IDENT "@" alias_pattern | pattern_atomic ;
pattern_atomic = "_" | INT_LIT | FLOAT_LIT | STRING_LIT | BOOL_LIT
                | "nil" | "unit"
                | IDENT
                | UPPER_IDENT [ "(" pattern { "," pattern } ")" ]
                | "(" pattern { "," pattern } ")"            (* tuple *)
                | "{" { IDENT ":" pattern "," } IDENT ":" pattern [ "," ] "}" ;

(* Types *)
type           = arrow_type ;
arrow_type     = atomic_type { "->" atomic_type } [ "!" effect_row ] ;
                 (* in nested (non-signature) arrow types `!` ALWAYS
                    introduces an effect row — drift item 5 *)
atomic_type    = UPPER_IDENT [ type_args ]    (* named: Int, Bool, String, Vec<T>, ... *)
                | "&" cap atomic_type         (* capability-qualified reference *)
                | "(" [ type { "," type } [ "," ] ] ")"
                | record_body ;
type_args      = "[" type { "," type } [ "," ] "]" ;

SEP            = newline | ";" ;
```

## 3. Stable extensions (Stable tier — change requires RFC + deprecation)

These productions extend the Core grammar. They are Stable: breaking changes
require an RFC and a deprecation cycle.

```
(* Effects (beyond IO) *)
decl          += effect_decl | handler_decl ;
effect_decl    = [ "pub" ] "effect" UPPER_IDENT "{" { effect_op SEP } "}" ;
effect_op      = IDENT ":" [ type ] "->" type ;
                 (* `next: -> Int` (no domain) and `write: String -> Unit` *)
handler_decl   = "handler" IDENT "=" "{" { handle_arm SEP } "}" ;
perform_expr   = "perform" UPPER_IDENT "." IDENT "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")" ;
primary       += perform_expr ;
primary       += handle_expr | catch_expr ;
handle_expr    = "handle" expr [ "with" [ IDENT ] ] "{" { handle_arm SEP } "}" ;
                (* `with` is optional; `with name` references a prior
                   `handler name = { ... }` declaration (src/parser.rs
                   parse_handle, consume_if(With) at :4182) *)
handle_arm     = [ "|" ] UPPER_IDENT "." IDENT "(" [ IDENT { "," IDENT } ] ")"
                 [ "resume" ] "=>" expr ;
                (* handle arms take BARE parameter names and accept NO
                   guards — unlike match, catch, and receive arms, which
                   accept `if` guards (drift item 7) *)
catch_expr     = expr "catch" ( expr | "{" { catch_arm ( SEP | "," ) } "}" )
                | "catch" expr ( expr | "{" { catch_arm ( SEP | "," ) } "}" ) ;
catch_arm      = [ "|" ] pattern [ "if" expr ] "=>" expr ;
                (* catch arms accept guards and optional `|` prefixes but
                   NOT the `case` keyword (drift item 6). Both forms
                   desugar to `match` on Ok/Error. RFC 0015 (Draft)
                   proposes removing `catch` entirely. *)
cap            = "val" | "iso" | "trn" | "ref" | "box" | "tag"
                | "lineariso" | "linear" ;
                (* capability annotations on parameters are PREFIX-only
                   (`val x: Int`); see §2 `param`. The postfix forms
                   `x: Int @ val` (params) and `let x: Int @ val` /
                   `let val x` (let bindings) are rejected
                   (drift round 1 / items 1-2). *)

(* Actors — the universal abstraction (SPEC2 Ch. 8) *)
decl          += actor_decl ;
actor_decl     = [ "pub" ] [ "persistent" ] ( "actor" | "entity" | "organization" )
                 IDENT [ type_params ] [ "implements" IDENT ]
                 "{" { actor_member SEP } "}" ;
actor_member   = state_field | behavior | initial_block
                | version_decl | events_block | apply_block | migration_block ;
                (* NO `fn` members in actor bodies (drift item 8).
                   `version`, `events`, `apply`, `migration` are contextual
                   keywords; src/parser.rs parse_actor :1003-1105. *)
state_field    = "state" [ state_model ] IDENT [ ":" type ] ( "=" | ":" ) expr ;
                (* the model keyword PRECEDES the field name and a default
                   value is required: `state durable count: Int = 0` *)
state_model    = "local" | "durable" | "event_sourced" | "crdt" ;
behavior       = "behavior" IDENT "(" [ param_list ] ")" [ sig_suffix ] block ;
initial_block  = "initial" IDENT "(" [ param_list ] ")" expr ;
version_decl   = "version" ":" INT_LIT ;
events_block   = "events" "{" { event_decl SEP } "}" ;
event_decl     = UPPER_IDENT [ "(" type ")" ] ;
apply_block    = "apply" "{" { apply_arm SEP } "}" ;
apply_arm      = [ "|" ] UPPER_IDENT "(" [ IDENT { "," IDENT } ] ")" "=>" expr ;
migration_block = "migration" block ;

(* Actor expressions *)
spawn_expr     = "spawn" [ "link" | "monitor" ] [ "@" expr ] IDENT
                 ( "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"
                 | "{" [ field_init { ( SEP | "," ) field_init } ] "}" )
                 [ "as" STRING_LIT ] ;
                (* NO capability annotation on spawn — `spawn iso Counter {}`
                   is rejected; there is no capability parsing in
                   parse_spawn (drift item 3; src/parser.rs :3839).
                   Positional constructor args XOR a field-init block;
                   field-init separators are `=` or `:`; `as "name"`
                   registers a stable identity. *)
send_expr      = "send" [ "remote" ] expr IDENT "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")"
                | "ask" expr IDENT "(" [ expr { "," expr } ] ")" ;
                (* keyword form, src/parser.rs parse_send_keyword :3721.
                   Postfix form `actor ! behavior(args)` (equivalently
                   `actor <- behavior(args)`) is a Pratt postfix operator
                   in parse_expr_with_prec (:2466, :2483); both desugar to
                   the same Expr::Send node. There is no `send a to b`
                   production. *)
receive_expr   = "receive" "{" { receive_arm ( SEP | "," ) } "}"
                 [ "after" expr "=>" expr ] ;
                (* the arm block may be EMPTY and the `after` body is any
                   expression (not only a block) — drift leniency 13,
                   admitted here as normative *)
primary       += spawn_expr | send_expr | receive_expr ;
receive_arm    = [ "|" ] IDENT "(" [ pattern { "," pattern } ] ")"
                 [ "if" expr ] "=>" expr ;
                (* receive arms bind PATTERNS and accept guards *)
```

## 4. Out of scope (Experimental tier)

The following surface is Experimental and deliberately **not** specified here.
It may change without RFC and is not part of the frozen or stable language:

- `workflow` and `agent` declarations (AI orchestration).
- `@backend(native|wasm)` actor annotation.
- `@tool(description: "...")` function annotation (LLM tool exposure).
- `extern "lib" { ... }` FFI blocks.
- `perform Python.call(...)` and the `python` feature surface.
- `@persistent` and the durable-execution syntax beyond the `@durable`
  state model (which is Stable).
- WASM/WIT integration, the `nula` package manager CLI grammar.

When an Experimental feature stabilizes, its productions are added to §3
under an RFC and removed from this list.

## 5. Parser conformance

The reference parser (`src/parser.rs`) must accept every string in the language
of §2 ∪ §3 and reject every string outside it (modulo Experimental §4). The
`conformance/grammar/` corpus contains positive cases (must parse) and negative
cases (must reject); `conformance/run.rs` executes them against the current
parser. A drift between this grammar and the parser is a bug in whichever was
not most recently updated under an RFC.

Known, documented exceptions — parser leniencies and parser bugs this grammar
deliberately does not admit — are enumerated in
`conformance/grammar/README.md` (drift log) with dispositions in
`docs/SPEC_RECONCILIATION.md`. As of the round-2 reconciliation these are:
unterminated block comments; mis-lexed `0b`/`0o`/underscore digit sequences;
`1.2.3` float member-chaining; case-insensitive name positions; empty
`catch {}` blocks; `resume(...)` outside a handler arm; trailing commas;
non-lvalue assignment targets; single-expression `#{...}` interpolation;
`type X = A B` without `|`; separator-less `let` chaining; `->` without a
type in lambdas; nameless `const`; and uppercase function names.
